readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|The ''wirdes'' that we clepen destinee.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 2580.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I was youngest,
And aye my ''wierd'' it was the hardest!</poem>
{{right|''Cospatrick'' (Child’s Ballads, I. 155).}}}}
{{smaller block|My ''weird'' maun be fulfilled.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xii.}}}}
{{smaller block|For the personification of ''Weird'' or Destiny, see Kemble, Saxons in England, i. 400: “it shall befall us as ''Weird'' decideth, the lord of every man.”
{{right|''C. Elton'', Origins of Eng. Hist., p. 386.}}}}
'''2.''' A prediction.
{{smaller block|<poem>His mither in her ''weirds''
Foretald his death at Troy.</poem>
{{right|''Poems in Buchan Dialect'', p. 18. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}
'''3.''' A spell; a charm. ''Scott.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')—'''4.''' That which comes to pass; a fact.
{{smaller block|After word comes ''weird''; fair fall them that call me Madam.
{{right|''Scotch Proverb.'' (''Jamieson.'')}}}}
'''5.''' The Fates personified. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Wo worth (quoth the ''Weirds'') the wights that thee wrought.
{{right|''Montgomerie'', in Watson’s Coll. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}

'''To dree one’s''' or '''a weird.''' See ''dree''<sup>1</sup>.

'''weird''' (wērd), ''a.'' [Not directly < ''weird'', ''n.'', but first in the phrase ''weird sisters'', an awkward expression, lit. ‘the fate sisters,’ appar. meant for ‘the Sister Fates’; but perhaps ''weird'' was thought to be an actual adjective meaning ‘fatal.’ No such adjective use is known in ME. The second use (def. 2) is due to an erroneous notion of the meaning of the phrase ''the weird sisters'', which has been taken to mean ‘the sisters who look witch-like or uncanny.’] '''1.''' Connected with fate or destiny; able to influence fate.

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|Makbeth and Banquho . . . met be ye gait thre women clothit in elrage and uncouth weid. They wer jugit be the pepill to be ''weird sisters.''
{{right|''Boethius'' (tr. by Bellenden).}}}}
'''2.''' Of or pertaining to witches or witchcraft; supernatural; hence, unearthly; suggestive of witches, witchery, or unearthliness; wild; uncanny.
{{smaller block|Out of the hardened clay and marl of the lake bottoms the elements are carving some of the ''weirdest'' scenery on the face of the earth.
{{right|''Geikie'', Geol. Sketches, ii. 8.}}
<poem>We heard the hawks at twilight play, . . .
The loon’s ''weird'' laughter far away.</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', Snow-Bound.}}}}

'''The weird sisters''', the Fates.
{{smaller block|<poem>The remanant hereof, quhat euer be it,
''The weird sisteris'' defendis that suld be wit.</poem>
{{right|''G. Douglas'', Æneid, iii.}}
<poem>I dreamt last night of ''the'' three ''weird sisters.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 1. 20.}}}}

'''weird''' (wērd), ''v. t.'' [Formerly also ''wierd''; < ''weird'', ''n.''] '''1.''' To destine; doom; change by witchcraft or sorcery.
{{smaller block|<poem>I ''weird'' ye to a fiery beast,
And relieved sall ye never be.</poem>
{{right|''Kempion'' (Child’s Ballads, I. 139).}}
Say, what hath forged thy ''wierded'' link of destiny with the House of Avenel?
{{right|''Scott'', Monastery, I. 231.}}}}
'''2.''' To warn solemnly; adjure.
{{smaller block|<poem>O byde at hame, my gude Lord Weire,
I ''weird'' ye byde at hame.</poem>
{{right|''Lammikin'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 308).}}}}

'''weirdless†''' (wērd′les), ''a.'' [< ''weird'' + ''-less''.] Ill-fated; luckless.
{{smaller block|<poem>Wae be to that ''weirdless'' wicht,</poem>}}

readings.c2s2:
''la'', will, wish, pleasure, + ''cuma'', one who comes, a comer: see ''will''<sup>1</sup> and ''come''. In ME. the word becomes confused with a similar form of Scand. origin, namely Icel. ''velkominn'' (= Sw. ''välkommen'' = Dan. ''velkommen'', welcome, lit. ‘well come,’ like F. ''bien venu''), < ''vel'', etc. (= E. ''well''), + ''kominn'', etc., = E. ''come'', pp.; but these forms were prob. orig. identical with the AS., D., and G. The adj. use is due to the position of the noun in the predicate, and in greeting, where it could still be regarded as a noun.] '''1.''' Gladly received for intercourse or entertainment; esteemed as one whose coming or presence is agreeable; held as doing well to come: as, a ''welcome'' guest or visitor; you are always ''welcome'' here; to make a visitor feel ''welcome''. {{smaller|Sometimes used elliptically as a word of greeting to a comer or comers: as, ''welcome'' home; bid our friends ''welcome''.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Welcome'', ffrendis; but I wolde frayne
How fare ȝe with that faire woman?</poem>
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 194.}}
<poem>Ye ’re ''welcome'' here, my young Redin,
For coal and candle licht.</poem>
{{right|''Young Redin'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 13).}}
Politeness and good breeding are equally necessary to make you ''welcome'' and agreeable in conversation and common life. {{right|''Chesterfield'', Letters.}}}}
'''2.''' Conferring gladness on receipt or presentation; such that its perception or acquisition gives pleasure; gladly received into knowledge or possession: as, ''welcome'' news; a ''welcome'' relief.
{{smaller block|A ''welcomer'' present to our master.
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Love’s Cure, v. 3.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Although my thoughts seem sad, they are ''welcome'' to me.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wife for a Month, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|They were a ''wellcum'' sight to see.
{{right|''Jamie Telfer'' (Child’s Ballads, VI. 114).}}}}

'''3.''' Gladly or willingly permitted, privileged, or the like; free to have, enjoy, etc.: as, you are ''welcome'' to do as you please; he is ''welcome'' to the money, or to all his honors.
{{smaller block|''Lod.'' Madam, good-night: I humbly thank your ladyship.
''Des.'' Your honour is most ''welcome.''
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iv. 3. 4.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1''' and '''2.''' Acceptable, agreeable, gratifying, pleasant.}}

'''welcome''' (wel′kum), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''welcomed'', ppr. ''welcoming''. [< ME. ''welcumen, wilcumen, wilcomen, wulcumen, wolcumen'', < AS. ''wilcumian'' (= G. ''be-willkommnen''), welcome, treat as a welcome guest, < ''wilcuma'', a welcome guest: see ''welcome, a.''] To greet the coming of with pleasure; salute with a welcome; receive gladly or joyfully: as, to ''welcome'' a friend, or the break of day.
{{smaller block|Thei . . . come to logres the thirde day, and ther were thei richely ''welcomed.'' {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 447.}}}}
{{smaller block|A brow unbent that seem’d to ''welcome'' woe.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 1509.}}}}

'''welcome''' (wel′kum), ''n.'' [< ''welcome, v.''] '''1.''' The act of bidding or making welcome; a kindly greeting to one coming.
{{smaller block|The camp receiv’d him with acclamations of joy and ''welcome.'' {{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''), Love’s Cure, i. 1.}}}}
